><mjx-mtr><mjx-mtd style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0px; padding-top: 0.4em;\"><mjx-tstrut></mjx-tstrut></mjx-mtd><mjx-mtd style=\"text-align: left; padding-left: 0px; padding-top: 0.4em;\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-n\"></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c34\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c33\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-tstrut></mjx-tstrut></mjx-mtd></mjx-mtr></mjx-itable></mjx-table></mjx-mtable></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.8em 0.8em 0.3em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi></mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mn>43.</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> </p>\n</div></div>",
  "name": "Question 47",
  "problem": "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[1,9].$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,9].$",
  "result": "Correct",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(1)Δ{x}_{1}+f(3)Δ{x}_{2}+f(6)Δ{x}_{3}+f(7)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 3+7\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+12+7+20 \\\\  & =43.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
